/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.34Gdm0/src/DC_Framework.tex:80:\noindent\textbf{The \texttt{DCFastQC} Algorithm.}
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.34Gdm0/src/DC_Framework.tex:81:% We call the divide-and-conquer algorithm, which runs \texttt{FastQC} on each of the graphs $G_i$, \texttt{DCFastQC}. 
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.34Gdm0/src/DC_Framework.tex:82:The pseudocode of \texttt{DCFastQC} is presented in Algorithm~\ref{alg:DCFastQC}. First, it reduces the graph to be the $\lceil \gamma\cdot (\theta - 1) \rceil$-core of $G$ (line 1). This is because every QC with size at least $\theta$ is within the $\lceil \gamma\cdot (\theta - 1) \rceil$-core of $G$~\cite{guo2020scalable}. Then, it computes the degeneracy ordering (line 2). Finally, it performs $n$ iterations (line 3 - 8). At the $i^{th}$ iteration, it constructs a smaller graph $G_i = G[V_i]$ (line 4), prunes the vertices from $V_i$ for MAX\_ROUND rounds, where MAX\_ROUND is a user parameter for controlling the trade-off between the workload and the effectiveness of the pruning techniques (line 5-6), and then runs \texttt{FastQC} on the refined graph (line 7-8).
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.34Gdm0/src/DC_Framework.tex:86:\noindent\textbf{Time complexity.} 
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.34Gdm0/src/DC_Framework.tex:87:% We summarize our algorithm \texttt{DCFastQC} with above framework in Algorithm~\ref{alg:DCFastQC}. Note that we reduce the graph as the $\lceil \gamma\cdot (\theta - 1) \rceil$-core of $G$ (line 1) based on the fact that every $\gamma$-QC with size at least $\theta$ is within the $\lceil \gamma\cdot (\theta - 1) \rceil$-core of $G$~\cite{guo2020scalable}. 
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.34Gdm0/src/DC_Framework.tex:88:% Similar as line 2, this can be done by core decomposition in polynomial time $O(|E|)$~\cite{batagelj2003m}. Besides, we conduct one-hop and two-hop pruning for MAX\_ROUND rounds and each round can be done efficiently in polynomial time which depends on the details of implementation. 
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.34Gdm0/src/DC_Framework.tex:89:The time cost is dominated by the part of invoking \texttt{FastQC} {\cheng $O(n)$ times}. 
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.34Gdm0/src/DC_Framework.tex:90:% It calls \texttt{FastQC} at most $O(n)$ rounds. 
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.34Gdm0/src/DC_Framework.tex:94:% Moreover, the largest upper bound $k_u(B)$ is reached at the initial branch and can be bounded by $k(\Delta/\gamma+1)=\lfloor \Delta\cdot (1-\gamma)/\gamma \rfloor+1$ based on Lemma~\ref{lemma:tight_bound}. 
