/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.34Gdm0/src/DC_Framework.tex:103:%\min\{\tau(\omega d), \tau(\omega/\gamma+1)\}=\min\{\lfloor\omega d (1-\gamma)+\gamma \rfloor, \lfloor \omega(1-\gamma)/\gamma +1 \rfloor\}$  
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.34Gdm0/src/DC_Framework.tex:105:%since (1) any branch $B$ has $S$ not empty and thus $\tau(\sigma(B))= \min\{\tau(|S\cup C|),\tau(d_{min}(B)/\gamma+1)\}$ based on Equation~(\ref{eq:fun_sigma}), (2) $|S\cup C|\leq \omega d$ since the size of a graph $G_i$ is bounded by $\omega d$, (3) $d_{min}(B)\leq \omega$ since $S$ must include a vertex $v_i$ ($1\leq i \leq n$) and thus $d_{min}(B)\leq \delta(v_i,S\cup C)\leq \delta(v_i,V_i)\leq \omega$ based on the property of degeneracy ordering, and thus (4) $\tau(\sigma(B))\leq k$.
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.34Gdm0/src/DC_Framework.tex:108:%$k=\tau(\omega/\gamma+1)=\lfloor \omega(1-\gamma)/\gamma +1 \rfloor$ {\cheng (which is based on Lemma~\ref{lemma:tight_bound})}. 
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.34Gdm0/src/DC_Framework.tex:110:We remark that {\cheng in practice}, \texttt{DCFastQC} is faster than \texttt{FastQC} since large graphs usually have $\omega$ and $d$ far smaller than the total number of vertices, {\cheng which will be verified in our experiments}.
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.34Gdm0/src/DC_Framework.tex:114:%With this framework, our algorithm called \texttt{DCFastQC+} can achieve better worst-case time complexity. Specifically, it has the worst-case running time $O(n\cdot \Delta d^2 \cdot \beta_{k}^{\Delta d})$ where $k=\lfloor \Delta\cdot \overline{\gamma}/\gamma \rfloor+1$ is the largest branching factor.
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.34Gdm0/src/DC_Framework.tex:118:% \noindent\textbf{Remark.} The divide-and-conquer technique has also been considered in \texttt{Quick+}. However, it is a basic version with only one-hop pruning, and does not consider the vertex ordering and two-hop pruning. Hence, our proposed technique would result in smaller subgraphs in theory and practice and thus performs better.
