{\cheng Let $H$ be a set of vertices.} We define $\Delta(H)$ {\cheng to be} the maximum number of disconnections of {\cheng a} vertex in $H$ {\cheng within $G[H]$}. Formally, we have
\begin{equation}
    \Delta(H)=\max_{v\in H} \overline{\delta}(v, H).
\end{equation}
% It {\cheng satisfies} one promising property which will be frequently used. Specifically, 
Consider a subset {\cheng $H_{sub}$} and a superset {\cheng $H_{sup}$} of $H$, we have 
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:delta}
    \Delta(H_{sub}) \leq \Delta(H) \leq \Delta(H_{sup})\  \text{for}\ H_{sub}\subseteq H \subseteq H_{sup}.
\end{equation}
This can be verified by {\cheng the fact that the set of} disconnections within a subgraph $G[H_{sub}]$ (resp. a supergraph $G[H_{sup}]$) is always a subset (resp. a superset) of that within $G[H]$. 
