## Use

```python
import gen_1011_6021 as g

inst = g.make_instance(**g.DIFFICULTY[g.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY], seed=7)
statement = g.render(inst)
answer = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + __import__("json").dumps(inst["answer"]) + "</answer>")
assert g.verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
```

After the bare transcript is regenerated, emit from the repository root with:

```bash
bash scripts/emit.sh 1011.6021
```

## Caveats

The family is easy with a sandbox: rowwise XOR plus Boolean interpolation solves
it in milliseconds. The 0/200,000 guess result is for the declared triangular
quadratic language, not for arbitrary JSON; the analytic bound is stronger but
still only an upper bound, not an exact shipping solution count. The attacks do
not include a general SAT solver because a generic satisfying assignment is not
necessarily expressible by the bounded polynomial certificate; the relevant
domain algorithm is exact quadratic list recovery and is fully disclosed.

The canonical key handles row/target reorderings and global source/target XOR
translations. It does not decide equivalence under every lower-triangular change
of Boolean coordinates; the ordered bit basis is part of the certificate
language, and full polynomial-map isomorphism was not attempted. Finally, the
paper proves worst-case NP-completeness, not hardness of this generated
distribution—hence Track B and the explicit reference algorithm.

