101:% %In the past, many studies devote to the problem of enumerating MQCs and contribute to a few algorithms including \texttt{Crochet}~\cite{pei2005mining,jiang2009mining}, \texttt{Cocain}~\cite{zeng2006coherent}, \texttt{Quick}~\cite{liu2008effective} and \texttt{Quick+}~\cite{khalil2022parallel,guo2020scalable}. They are all \emph{branch-and-bound} algorithms and based on a conventional branching strategy that we call \emph{Set-Enumeration (SE) branching}~\cite{khalil2022parallel,guo2020scalable}. Recent progress~\cite{khalil2022parallel,guo2020scalable} focuses on sharpening the pruning techniques employed in the algorithms so as to practically prune more branches that cannot hold any MQC. Nevertheless, all of them have the efficiency highly rely on the practical performance of deployed pruning techniques and have the worst-case time complexity of $O^*(2^n)$ where $n$ is the number of vertices in the given graph and $O^*$ suppresses polynomials. 
105:% %We note that solely deploying pruning techniques is typically not enough to break $O^*(2^n)$ since those pruning rules may not be able to prune any branch in the worst case without explicit theoretical guarantee on their performance. To tackle this issue, recent studies~\cite{zhou2020enumerating,yu2022maximum,chen2021computing} resort to design new branching strategies (e.g., pivot-based branching), which better favour deployed pruning techniques compared with the conventional one. Specifically, with newly designed one, we can guarantee at least a certain number of formed sub-branches to be pruned even in the worst case.
