118:% \noindent\textbf{New methods.} To improve, we propose to \emph{co-design new pruning techniques and new branching strategies} for the following considerations. 
120:% First, solely sharpening pruning techniques is not enough to break $O^*(2^n)$ as existing studies~\cite{pei2005mining,jiang2009mining,zeng2006coherent,liu2008effective,khalil2022parallel,guo2020scalable} do since they cannot prune any branch in the worst case without explicit guarantee. 
122:% Second, for those BB algorithms~\cite{zhou2020enumerating,yu2022maximum,chen2021computing} designed for enumerating maximal subgraph structures that satisfy the hereditary property (e.g., clique), with the carefully designed branching strategies, their pruning techniques (relying on hereditary property) would prune at least a certain number of formed branches even in the worst case and thus have the worst-case time complexity strictly smaller than $O^*(2^n)$. This motivates us to consider other possible branching strategies for enumerating MQCs, though hereditary property no longer holds. However, we cannot find any new branching strategy which can theoretically boost the performance of existing pruning techniques for enumerating MQCs.
124:% Therefore, both new pruning techniques and new branching strategies are needed, which should be co-designed since they would work together to break $O^*(2^n)$ as discussed above.
125:% %when designing branching strategies, we must consider deployed pruning techniques since they are designed to boost the performance of pruning techniques. Moreover, we cannot find any new branching strategy which can theoretically boost the performance of existing pruning techniques.
127:% %solely designing branching strategies without considering pruning techniques is useless since they are  boost the performance of pruning techniques. Third, we cannot find any branching strategy which can theoretically guarantee the performance of existing pruning techniques. 
130:% Follow our co-design proposal, we first introduce a novel necessary condition for a branch holding QCs, which is developed on a 2-dimensional space, namely \emph{SD space}. For those branches that violate the condition, they hold no QCs and thus can be pruned. Based on this, we develop the new pruning techniques. 
132:% We then introduce a symmetric branching of the SE branching, called \emph{Symmetric-SE (Sym-SE) branching}, which can boost the performance of our pruning techniques. Specifically, with Sym-SE branching, our pruning techniques would prune more formed sub-branches in both practice and theory.  
136:% We finally introduce a new BB algorithm called \texttt{FastQC}, which is based on the co-designed pruning techniques and branching strategies. \texttt{FastQC} has its worst-case time complexity $O(n\cdot d \cdot \alpha_k^n)$ (i.e., $O^*(\alpha_k^n)$) where $\alpha_k$ is strictly smaller than 2 and depends on the value of $k$, e.g., $\alpha_k=1.414$ when $k=1$. It achieves a theoretical breakthrough since existing algorithms have the worst-case running time $O^*(2^n)$~\cite{pei2005mining,jiang2009mining,zeng2006coherent,liu2008effective,khalil2022parallel,guo2020scalable}.
138:In addition, we adapt a \emph{divide-and-conquer (DC)} strategy for boosting the efficiency and scalability of \texttt{FastQC}.
140:Basically, it divides the whole graph into multiple smaller ones and then runs \texttt{FastQC} on each of them. Furthermore, we develop some new pruning techniques to shrink the constructed smaller graphs for better efficiency. In summary, the resulting algorithm called \texttt{DCFastQC} would invoke \texttt{FastQC} multiple times, each on a smaller graph (compared with the original graph), and thus the scalability is improved.
