589:%Let $\tau(B)=\tau(\sigma(B))$. We remark that there are two special cases. First, when $\sigma(B)<|S|$ (note that this could occur in case of a small value of $d_{min}(B)$), branch $B$ would hold no QCs based on Lemma~\ref{lemma:tight_bound}. Second, when $\tau(B)>\Delta(S\cup C)$, branch $B$ holds a QC $G[S\cup C]$ since (1) $\tau(\cdot)$ is a non-decreasing function and (2) thus $G[S\cup C]$ is a QC due to $\Delta(S\cup C)\leq \tau(B)\leq \tau(|S\cup C|)$ and Lemma~\ref{lemma:TD_Property2}. For simplicity, we rule out these two special cases and further require $|S|\leq \sigma(B)\leq |S\cup C|$ and $\tau(B)<\Delta(S\cup C)$, which actually restricts the position of the top-right point $(\sigma(B),\tau(B))$ of the above region. 
