691:% \noindent\textbf{Discussion.} We remark that the problem of determine whether a branch $B=(S,C,D)$ (or one of its sub-branches) hold a QC is NP-hard (due to the non-hereditary property of QC), for which we put the detailed proof in the technical report. Therefore, we cannot find any necessary and sufficient condition that can be verified in the polynomial time. 
698:Consider a branch $B = (S, C, D)$. We first check if the necessary condition C1\&2, i.e., $\Delta(S) \le \tau(\sigma(B))$, is satisfied. If no, we can prune $B$ directly; If yes, while we cannot prune $B$ immediately, we may be able to \emph{refine} $B$ with the information $\tau(\sigma(B))$ by removing some vertices from $C$. We then \emph{re-check} the condition for the refined branch, which we denote by $B'$, and prune it if the condition is not satisfied. The rationale is that with some vertices removed from the candidate set $C$, the necessary condition would become less likely to be satisfied and correspondingly the branch can be pruned more likely, as will be explained later. In fact, we can repeat this process until either (1) the branch is pruned; or (2) the branch cannot be refined further. We provide the details as follows.
701:\noindent\textbf{Refining a Branch.} With the information of $\tau(\sigma(B))$, we can possibly refine $B$ by removing from $C$ some vertices as follows.
703:    \item \textbf{Refinement Rule 1.} Remove from $C$ those vertices $v$ with $\Delta(S\cup\{v\})> \tau(\sigma(B))$
704:    \item \textbf{Refinement Rule 2.} Remove from $C$ those vertices $v$ with $\delta(v,S\cup C)< \theta-\tau(\sigma(B))$
708:$\Delta(S\cup\{v\})\leq \Delta(H)\leq \tau(|H|)\leq \tau(\sigma(B))$, which contradicts to $\Delta(S\cup \{v\})>\tau(\sigma(B))$.
709:Here, $\Delta(S\cup\{v\})\leq \Delta(H)$ is because $S\cup \{v\} \subseteq H$ (by assumption), $\Delta(H)\leq \tau(|H|)$ is because $G[H]$ is a QC, and $\tau(|H|)\leq \tau(\sigma(B))$ is because $|H| \leq \sigma(B)$ (based on Lemma~\ref{lemma:tight_bound}) and $\tau(\cdot)$ is non-decreasing.
711:For Rule (2), {\cheng it is because} only those QCs with the size at least $\theta$ are to be found. For each vertex $v$ in such a large QC $G[H]$ under branch $B$, we have $\delta(v, S\cup C)\geq \delta(v, H) = |H|- \overline{\delta}(v,H)\geq |H|-\Delta(H)\geq |H|-\tau(|H|)\geq |H|- \tau(\sigma(B))\geq \theta-\tau(\sigma(B))$.
714:\noindent\textbf{Re-checking the Necessary Condition.} Suppose the branch $B = (S, C, D)$ has been refined to $B' = (S, C', D)$ with $C' \subset C$. We note that $\tau(\sigma(B'))$ would be possibly smaller than $\tau(\sigma(B))$. This is because $C' \subset C$ implies $d_{min}(B') \le d_{min}(B)$, which further implies $\sigma(B') \le \sigma(B)$, and $\tau(\cdot)$ is non-decreasing. Therefore, we can re-check the necessary condition for branch $B'$, i.e., $\Delta(S) \le \tau(\sigma(B'))$, which is less likely to be satisfied than that for branch $B$, i.e., $\Delta(S) \le \tau(\sigma(B))$, given that $\tau(\sigma(B')) \le \tau(\sigma(B))$. If the condition is not satisfied, we prune branch $B'$.
718:\noindent\textbf{Repeated Process and Stopping Criterion.} In the case that the refined branch $B'$ cannot be pruned. We can repeat the process of refining $B'$ (based on the information of $\tau(\sigma(B'))$) and re-checking the condition for the refined branch. We stop the process until either (1) a refined branch is pruned or (2) a branch cannot be refined any further (i.e., no vertices can be removed from the candidate set).
724:%Consider the necessary condition for a branch $B=(S,C,D)$, i.e., $\Delta(S)\leq \tau(\sigma(B))$. We observe that (1) branch $B$ can be refined (i.e., remove from $C$ some vertices that cannot appear in any QC) by some pruning rules developed based on $\tau(\sigma(B))$ and (2) for the refined $B$, the necessary condition can be tightened by deriving a smaller value of $\tau(\sigma(B))$ (note that $\sigma(B)$ tends to get smaller as $C$ narrows based on Equation~(\ref{eq:fun_sigma})).
727:% \noindent\textbf{Pruning rules.} We firstly introduce the following pruning rules for refining branch $B$ based on $\tau(\sigma(B))$.
730:%     \item Remove from $C$ those vertices $v$ that $\Delta(S\cup\{v\})> \tau(\sigma(B))$
731:%     \item Remove from $C$ those vertices $v$ that $\delta(v,S\cup C)\leq \theta-\tau(\sigma(B))$. 
733:% For Rule (1), every QC $G[H]$ under branch $B$ is a superset of $S$ and thus cannot include such a vertex $v$ since otherwise $G[S\cup \{v\}]$ would have $\Delta(S\cup\{v\})\leq \Delta(H)\leq \tau(|H|)\leq \tau(\sigma(B))$ which contradicts to $\Delta(S\cup \{v\})>\tau(\sigma(B))$ (note that $\Delta(S\cup\{v\})\leq \Delta(H)$ based on Equation~(\ref{eq:delta}), $\Delta(H)\leq \tau(|H|)$ since Lemma~\ref{lemma:TD_Property2}, and $\tau(|H|)\leq \tau(\sigma(B))$ since QC $G[H]$ has $|H|\leq \sigma(B)$ based on the Lemma~\ref{lemma:tight_bound} and $\tau(\cdot)$ is non-decreasing).
735:% For Rule (2), only those QCs with size at least $\theta$ could be yielded and every vertex $v$ in such a large QC $G[H]$ under branch $B$ has $H\subseteq S\cup C$ and thus $\delta(v, S\cup C)\geq \delta(v,H)= |H|-\overline{\delta}(v,H)\geq |H|-\Delta(H)\geq |H|-\tau(|H|)\geq |H|- \tau(\sigma(B))\geq \theta-\tau(\sigma(B))$ which can be verified similarly as above.
738:% \noindent\textbf{Stopping criteria.} After refining branch $B$, the necessary conditions (i.e., value of $\tau(\sigma(B))$) can be updated. Moreover, we iteratively and alternatively conduct these two procedures until (1) the branch violates the necessary condition, i.e., $\Delta(S)>\tau(\sigma(B))$ (for which we terminate the branch without branching) and (2) $\tau(\sigma(B))$ remains unchangeable.
