766:% However, the situation deteriorates when solving our problem due to the non-hereditary graph property of quasi-clique. To be specific, there could be some $\gamma$-MQCs to be found in a current branch even if it has the partial set not a $\gamma$-QC (since a superset of a non-$\gamma$-QC could be a $\gamma$-QC due to Property~\ref{property-non-hereditary}). Hence, we cannot prune those branches with the partial set not a $\gamma$-QC and the search space boundary becomes vague. This indicates the difficulties to design effective pruning rules and branching strategies based on Property~\ref{property:tree-based-space} under the tree-based representation. 
815:% Compared with the tree-based representation which describes the search space as a set of branches (i.e., triples) organized as a tree structure, TDim has unique advantage for our problem. Specifically, it exploits two intrinsic features of a subgraph (i.e., number of vertices and disconnections inside) which relate to the definition of QC but not be examined in tree-based representation. Based on this, it derives tractable boundaries of the search and solution space to better help us to design effective pruning rules (for refining search space of a branch in Section~\ref{subsec:progressive_bound}) and branching strategies (for reducing the number of formed branches in Section~\ref{subsec:Sym-PivSE}). In fact, our new algorithm \texttt{FastQC} designed based on TDim would have the worst-case time complexity strictly smaller than that of \texttt{Quick+} (details will be introduced in Section~\ref{subsec:summary_fastqc}).
825:% Consider a branch $B=(S,C,D)$, we observe that Equation~(\ref{eq:search_space_bound}) gives a loose boundary of the search space since it assumes the size of a possible $\gamma$-QC up to $|S\cup C|$ (note that this boundary is reached only when $G[S\cup C]$ is a $\gamma$-QC). Hence, it rarely helps to design pruning rules which refine branch $B$ physically, e.g., removing some vertices from $C$. To improve, we propose a progressive bounded method which iteratively and alternatively tightens the theoretical search space of $B$ under TDim (i.e., by a smaller square region) and the physical branch $(S,C,D)$ (i.e., by pruning rules). Below, we elaborate on the details of this method.
