   450	\noindent\textbf{Necessary Condition (Summary).} In summary, if a branch $B$ holds a QC $G[H]$, then the point of $G[H]$ in the SD space must reside in both Region $R_1$ and Region $R_2'$.
   451	% as specified in Equation~(\ref{eq:condition1}) and Equation~(\ref{eq:condition2}), respectively. 
   452	It further implies that the intersection of the two regions, which we denote by $R_{1\&2} = R_1 \cap R_2'$, is non-empty. We use this as the necessary condition for branch $B$ to hold a QC and shall show that it can be verified efficiently in $O(d)$ time. 
   453	Specifically, we have the following necessary condition.
   454	
   455	\medskip
   456	\noindent\fbox{%
   457	    \parbox{0.47\textwidth}{%
   458	       \textbf{Condition C1\&2:} If a branch $B$ holds a QC, then $R_{1\&2} = R_1 \cap R_2'$ is non-empty.
   459	    }%
   460	}
   461	\medskip
   462	
   463	For illustration, we show the case where the necessary condition C1\&2 is satisfied in Figure~\ref{fig:SD_Exp2}(a) and the case where C1\&2 is not satisfied in Figure~\ref{fig:SD_Exp2}(b). 
   464	
   465	We notice that the necessary condition C1\&2, i.e., Region $R_{1\&2}$ is non-empty, is equivalent to that $\Delta(S) \leq \tau(\sigma(B))$. This is because when $\Delta(S) \leq \tau(\sigma(B))$, regions $R_1$ and $R_2'$ would intersect and when $\Delta(S) > \tau(\sigma(B))$, the regions would not intersect, and vice versa, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:SD_Exp2}. 
   466	% An illustration of the two cases is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:SD_Exp2}(a) and (b). 
