   730	\subsection{A {\cheng Symmetric} Branching Strategy {\cheng of SE Branching}: {\cheng Sym-SE Branching}}
   731	\label{subsec:Sym-PivSE}
   732	
   733	Consider a branch $B=(S,C,D)$, {\cheng which} satisfies the necessary condition C1\&2, i.e., $\Delta(S)\leq \tau(\sigma(B))$, after the 
   734	{\cheng the progressive process of refining a branch and re-checking the necessary condition.}
   735	% progressively re-checking procedure. 
   736	We need to create sub-branches from branch $B$ via branching. 
   737	%
   738	{\cheng One option is to adopt the SE branching, which is defined in Equation~(\ref{eq:SE_branching}), as Quick+ does. Another option is to adopt a branching {\cheng that is \emph{symmetric} to} the SE branching, called \emph{Sym-SE branching}, which is defined as follows.
   739	%
   740	It creates $(|C|+1)$ sub-branches from $B$, namely $B_1, B_2, ..., B_{|C|+1}$. Branch $B_i$ ($1\le i\le |C|+1$) covers those vertex sets, each (1) including all vertices in $S \cup \{v_1,v_2,...,v_{i-1}\}$; (2) excluding all vertices in $D \cup \{v_i\}$. Formally, branch $B_i$ is defined as follows.
   741	\begin{equation}
   742	    S_i\!=\!S\cup \{v_1,v_2,...,v_{i-1}\},\ D_i\!=\!D\cup\{v_i\},\ C_i\!=\!C-\{v_1,v_2,...,v_i\}.
   743	\label{eq:sym-se}
   744	\end{equation}
   745	Here, $v_0$ in branch $B_1$ and $v_{|C|+1}$ in branch $B_{|C|+1}$ are both fictitious.
   746	An illustration of the Sym-SE branching is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:illustration_branching}(b).
   747	%
   748	{\cheng The two branching methods are symmetric because as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:illustration_branching}, (1) for SE branching, branches $B_1$, ..., $B_i$ \emph{include} vertex $v_i$ while the remaining branches \emph{exclude} vertex $v_i$ and (2) for Sym-SE branching, branches $B_1$, ..., $B_i$ \emph{exclude} vertex $v_i$ while the remaining branches \emph{include} vertex $v_i$.
   749	}
   750	
   751	We note that a symmetric branching of the BK branching, which is called \emph{Sym-BK branching}, has also been explored for enumerating maximal subgraphs that satisfy the hereditary property~\cite{yu2022maximum,zhou2020enumerating}. Sym-SE branching and Sym-BK branching share the way of forming branches. The difference is that Sym-BK branching can prune some of the formed branches based on the hereditary property. In contrast, Sym-SE does not require the subgraphs to be enumerated to satisfy the hereditary property, and correspondingly it cannot prune some formed branches as Sym-BK does.
   752	% To this end, we introduce a new branching strategy, namely \emph{symmetric SE (Sym-SE) branching}, which is adapted from the Sym-BK branching~\cite{yu2022maximum,zhou2020enumerating} and more suitable for our problem. 
   753	% Specifically, it would create $(|C|+1)$ sub-branches at $B$. Each of them $B_i=(S_i,C_i,D_i)$ holds $S$ and $\{v_1,v_2,...,v_{i-1}\}$ in $S_i$ and holds $D$ and $v_i$ in $D_i$. Note that $v_0$ in branch $B_1$ and $v_{|C|+1}$ in branch $B_{|C|+1}$ are both empty. Formally, for $1\leq i\leq |C|+1$, we have
   754	% \begin{equation}
   755	%     S_i\!=\!S\cup \{v_1,v_2,...,v_{i-1}\},\ D_i\!=\!D\cup\{v_i\},\ C_i\!=\!C-\{v_1,v_2,...,v_i\}.
   756	% \end{equation}
   757	%
   758	
   759	We show that the necessary condition C1\&2 defined in the SD space would work more effectively when the Sym-SE branching is used than when the SE branching is used. The reason is two-fold. \underline{First}, a created branch $B_i$ by Sym-SE branching would have a larger chance to violate the necessary condition C1\&2, i.e., $\Delta(S_i) > \tau(\sigma(B))$~\footnote{{\cheng We note that for sub-branches, we use a looser condition than the necessary condition for branch $B_i$, i.e., $\Delta(S_i) {\cheng \le} \tau(\sigma(B_i))$. 
   760	% Note that we have $\tau(\sigma(B)) \ge \tau(\sigma(B_i))$, which can be verified based on the fact that $d_{min}(B) \geq d_{min}(B_i)$ and $\tau(\cdot)$ is non-decreasing.
   761	}}, and be pruned, when $i$ gets larger. This is due to the fact that $S_i$ involves $|S|+i-1$ vertices, and correspondingly we have $\Delta(S_i)$ increase with $i$. 
   762	% We note that $\tau(\sigma(B)) \ge \tau(\sigma(B_i))$ can be verified based on the fact that $d_{min}(B) \geq d_{min}(B_i)$ and $\tau(\cdot)$ is non-decreasing.
   763	%
   764	\underline{Second}, if a branch $B_i$ by Sym-SE branching violates the necessary condition (i.e., $\Delta(S_i)>\tau(\sigma(B))$) and can be pruned, then all other branches $B_j$ following $B_i$ with $j > i$ would violate the necessary condition (i.e., $\Delta(S_j)>\tau(\sigma(B))$) and can be pruned also. This is due to the fact that the partial set $S_j$ is always a superset of $S_i$ and thus we have $\Delta(S_j) \geq \Delta(S_i) > \tau(\sigma(B))$.
   765	%
   766	
   767	For illustration, consider Figure~\ref{fig:SD_branching}(a) for Sym-SE branching, where when a branch $B_i$ can be pruned, any branch $B_j$ with $j>i$ can also be pruned. Consider Figure~\ref{fig:SD_branching}(b) for SE branching, where a branch $B_i$ can be pruned does not imply that any branch $B_j$ with $j>i$ can be pruned.
   768	}
