   778	\smallskip
   779	\noindent\textbf{Ordering of Vertices in $C$.}
   780	Sym-SE branching implicitly uses an ordering of vertices in $C$, which we specify as follows.
   781	}
   782	% By following some existing works~\cite{yu2022maximum,zhou2020enumerating}, we further improve the Sym-SE branching by determining the ordering of vertices in $C$. 
   783	{\cheng We} find a smaller subset $C'$ of $C$ and put them before other vertices in the ordering such that including them to the partial set collectively would {\cheng cause} one sub-branch {\cheng to} violate the {\cheng necessary} condition (i.e., $\Delta(S\cup C')>\tau(\sigma(B))$) and thus {\cheng this branch and also the} following sub-branches can be pruned. Specifically, the ordering is defined based on a vertex $\hat{v}$ called \emph{pivot}, which is selected {\cheng from those vertices in $S\cup C$ that} have more than $\tau(\sigma(B))$ disconnections among $S\cup C$, i.e., $\overline{\delta}(\hat{v},S\cup C)>\tau(\sigma(B))$.
   784	%
   785	We define
   786	\begin{equation}
   787	    a=\tau(\sigma(B))-\overline{\delta}(\hat{v},S)\ \text{and}\ b=\overline{\delta}(\hat{v},C),
   788	\end{equation}
   789	where $a$ denotes the largest possible number of vertices that can be included from $\overline{\Gamma}(\hat{v},C)$ to $S$ without violating the necessary condition. Note that $a<b$ since $b-a=\overline{\delta}(\hat{v},S\cup C)-\tau(\sigma(B))>0$.
   790	%
   791	We put those vertices in $\overline{\Gamma}(\hat{v},C)$ before others in the ordering. There are two cases.
   792	
   793	\noindent\underline{Case 1}: $\hat{v}\in S$. We define the ordering of vertices in $C$ as follows.
   794	\begin{equation}
   795	    \label{sym-pivse-case1}
   796	    \langle v_1,v_2,...,v_b,v_{b+1},...,v_{|C|} \rangle,
   797	    %\langle v_1,v_2,...,v_b,v_{b+1},...,v_{|C|} \rangle,
   798	\end{equation}
   799	where the first $b$ vertices, namely $v_1,v_2,...,v_b$, are from $\overline{\Gamma}(\hat{v},C)$ in any order, and the others are from $\Gamma(\hat{v},C)$ in any order. Then, branch $B_{a+2}$ would violate the necessary condition since $\Delta(S_{a+2})\geq \overline{\delta}(\hat{v},S_{a+2})  = \overline{\delta}(\hat{v}, S\cup \{v_1, v_2, ..., v_{a+1}\}) = \overline{\delta}(\hat{v}, S) + \overline{\delta}(\hat{v}, \{v_1, v_2, ..., v_{a+1}\}) = \overline{\delta}(\hat{v},S)+a+1=\tau(\sigma(B))+1$. Consequently, the branches $B_{a+2}, B_{a+3},...,B_{|C|+1}$ violate the necessary condition and can be pruned. %Besides, those vertices $v_{a+2},v_{a+3},...,v_{b}$ can be removed from the candidate set of branch $B_{a+1}$ explicitly since $\Delta(S_{a+1}\cup\{v_i\})>\tau(\sigma(B))$ for any $a+2 \leq i\leq b$ which can be similarly verified as Pruning rule (1).
   800	%
