/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/exp.tex:362:\noindent\textbf{Performance study of \texttt{DC} on reducing graph size.} \texttt{DC} framework divides the whole graph into multiple smaller ones, which is orthogonal to the branch-and-bound algorithms. We thus solely study the effect of \texttt{DC} on reducing graph size and compare three different versions, namely (1) \texttt{DC2.0}: the full version of proposed \texttt{DC}, (2) \texttt{DC1.0}: the full version without the two-hop pruning, and (3) \texttt{NoDC}: without \texttt{DC} framework. We report the average size of produced smaller subgraphs $G_i$ in Figure~\ref{fig:performance_DC} for varying $\theta$ and $\gamma$. Note that \texttt{DC1.0} is similar as the one proposed in \texttt{Quick+}~\cite{khalil2022parallel} and \texttt{NoQC} has the average size of $G_i$ as the size of original input graph.
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/exp.tex:364:First, the reduced graph $G_i$ produced by \texttt{DC2.0} is smaller than (around 1\%) that by \texttt{DC1.0} and (around 0.01\% of) the original graph (i.e., Enron). Second, \texttt{DC} produces smaller graphs as $\theta$ {\cheng or} $\gamma$ grow.
