/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/intro2.tex:88:% %Hereditary property is important when designing efficient algorithms for enumerating maximal cohesive subgraphs.
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/intro2.tex:89:% %, e.g., even determining whether a QC is maximal is NP-hard~\cite{sanei2018enumerating}.  
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/intro2.tex:96:% Nevertheless, these pruning rules cannot prune any branch in the worst case and all of them have the worst-case time complexity of $O^*(2^n)$ where $n$ is the number of vertices in the given graph and $O^*$ suppresses polynomials.
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/intro2.tex:98:% %In the past, many studies devote to the problem of enumerating MQCs and contribute to a few algorithms including \texttt{Crochet}~\cite{pei2005mining,jiang2009mining}, \texttt{Cocain}~\cite{zeng2006coherent}, \texttt{Quick}~\cite{liu2008effective} and \texttt{Quick+}~\cite{khalil2022parallel,guo2020scalable}. They are all \emph{branch-and-bound} algorithms and based on a conventional branching strategy that we call \emph{Set-Enumeration (SE) branching}~\cite{khalil2022parallel,guo2020scalable}. Recent progress~\cite{khalil2022parallel,guo2020scalable} focuses on sharpening the pruning techniques employed in the algorithms so as to practically prune more branches that cannot hold any MQC. Nevertheless, all of them have the efficiency highly rely on the practical performance of deployed pruning techniques and have the worst-case time complexity of $O^*(2^n)$ where $n$ is the number of vertices in the given graph and $O^*$ suppresses polynomials. 
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/intro2.tex:104:% %However, not all pruning techniques can be improved in this way. Recent studies mainly focus on those for enumerating maximal subgraphs that satisfy \emph{hereditary} property, e.g., $k$-(bi)plexes~\cite{zhou2020enumerating,yu2022maximum} and $s$-defective cliques~\cite{chen2021computing}. This is mainly due to the profit of hereditary properties, e.g., any superset of a non-$k$-plex is also a non-$k$-plex, which allows the pruning technique prune a collection of branches and favour some special branching strategy.
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/intro2.tex:119:% Second, for those BB algorithms~\cite{zhou2020enumerating,yu2022maximum,chen2021computing} designed for enumerating maximal subgraph structures that satisfy the hereditary property (e.g., clique), with the carefully designed branching strategies, their pruning techniques (relying on hereditary property) would prune at least a certain number of formed branches even in the worst case and thus have the worst-case time complexity strictly smaller than $O^*(2^n)$. This motivates us to consider other possible branching strategies for enumerating MQCs, though hereditary property no longer holds. However, we cannot find any new branching strategy which can theoretically boost the performance of existing pruning techniques for enumerating MQCs.
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/intro2.tex:133:% We finally introduce a new BB algorithm called \texttt{FastQC}, which is based on the co-designed pruning techniques and branching strategies. \texttt{FastQC} has its worst-case time complexity $O(n\cdot d \cdot \alpha_k^n)$ (i.e., $O^*(\alpha_k^n)$) where $\alpha_k$ is strictly smaller than 2 and depends on the value of $k$, e.g., $\alpha_k=1.414$ when $k=1$. It achieves a theoretical breakthrough since existing algorithms have the worst-case running time $O^*(2^n)$~\cite{pei2005mining,jiang2009mining,zeng2006coherent,liu2008effective,khalil2022parallel,guo2020scalable}.
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/intro2.tex:146:    {\cheng has the} worst-case time complexity of $O(n\cdot d\cdot \alpha_k^n)$ with $\alpha_k<2$,
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/intro2.tex:147:    {\cheng which breaks the long-standing bottleneck time complexity of $O^*(2^n)$~\footnote{{\cheng We note that there has been some recent progress of improving the time complexity for enumerating some subgraphs that satisfy the hereditary property (e.g., $k$-plex and $s$-defective clique)~\cite{zhou2020enumerating,chen2021computing}, but they cannot be used for our problem since QCs do not satisfy the property. To our best knowledge, this is the first breakthrough of worst-case time complexity of enumerating subgraphs that do not satisfy the hereditary property.}}.
