/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/framework5.tex:566:% For Rule (2), only those QCs with size at least $\theta$ could be yielded and every vertex $v$ in such a large QC $G[H]$ under branch $B$ has $H\subseteq S\cup C$ and thus $\delta(v, S\cup C)\geq \delta(v,H)= |H|-\overline{\delta}(v,H)\geq |H|-\Delta(H)\geq |H|-\tau(|H|)\geq |H|- \tau(\sigma(B))\geq \theta-\tau(\sigma(B))$ which can be verified similarly as above.
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/framework5.tex:588:% We note that \texttt{Quick+} recognizes the search space (i.e., the set of possible $\gamma$-MQCs) as a tree structure (e.g., SE-tree), which is traditionally used among many branch-and-bound algorithms for enumerating maximal subgraphs~\cite{khalil2022parallel,zhou2020enumerating,yu2022maximum,guo2020scalable,gao2022exact,bron1973algorithm}. This tree-based representation explicitly exploits the most promising property of a branch-and-bound algorithm, as shown below and in Figure~\ref{fig:set-enumeration}.
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/framework5.tex:595:% Property~\ref{property:tree-based-space} plays an important role during the algorithm design, especially for those problems of enumerating maximal subgraphs that satisfy a hereditary graph property, e.g., clique. For example, if a current branch $B=(S,C,D)$ has the partial set not a clique, any sub-branch of the current one does so and thus can be pruned since (1) its partial set is a superset of $S$ due to Property~\ref{property:tree-based-space} and (2) any superset of a non-clique is not a clique due to the hereditary property. Essentially, this figures out the boundary of search space which helps us determine whether a branch hold desired subgraphs quickly.
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/framework5.tex:597:% However, the situation deteriorates when solving our problem due to the non-hereditary graph property of quasi-clique. To be specific, there could be some $\gamma$-MQCs to be found in a current branch even if it has the partial set not a $\gamma$-QC (since a superset of a non-$\gamma$-QC could be a $\gamma$-QC due to Property~\ref{property-non-hereditary}). Hence, we cannot prune those branches with the partial set not a $\gamma$-QC and the search space boundary becomes vague. This indicates the difficulties to design effective pruning rules and branching strategies based on Property~\ref{property:tree-based-space} under the tree-based representation. 
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/framework5.tex:631:%     A subgraph $G[H]$ is said to be a $\gamma$-QC iff  $\overline{\delta}_m(H)\leq k(\gamma,|H|)$ where $k(\gamma,|H|)$ (simply $k(|H|)$) is a function $\lfloor (1-\gamma) |H| + \gamma \rfloor$. 
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/framework5.tex:634:%     This can be proved by definition since every vertex $v$ in a $\gamma$-QC $G[H]$ has $\overline{\delta}(v,H)\leq |H|- \delta(v,H)\leq |H| - \lceil \gamma(|H|-1) \rceil\leq |H| + \lfloor \gamma(1-|H|) \rfloor=\lfloor |H|+\gamma(1-|H|) \rfloor=k(|H|)$.
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/framework5.tex:639:% \noindent\textbf{Search and solution space under TDim.} We are now ready to figure out the search and solution space under TDim based on Lemma~\ref{lemma:TD_Property1} and Lemma~\ref{lemma:TD_Property2}. They are depicted as a region of points in Figure~\ref{fig:TD_example}(a). Specifically, the universal search space is the square region $(0,0)\times (n,\overline{\delta}_m(V))$ since any possible MQC $G[H]$ is a subgraph of $G=(V,E)$ and thus has $|H|\leq n$ and $\overline{\delta}_m(H)\leq \overline{\delta}_m(V)$ based on Lemma~\ref{lemma:TD_Property1}. The solution space (i.e., set of all $\gamma$-MQCs) is the region bounded by $k(\cdot)$ function according to Lemma~\ref{lemma:TD_Property2}. Moreover, for a branch $B=(S,C,D)$, the search space of $B$, as depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:TD_example}(b),  is a region of the square
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/framework5.tex:646:% Compared with the tree-based representation which describes the search space as a set of branches (i.e., triples) organized as a tree structure, TDim has unique advantage for our problem. Specifically, it exploits two intrinsic features of a subgraph (i.e., number of vertices and disconnections inside) which relate to the definition of QC but not be examined in tree-based representation. Based on this, it derives tractable boundaries of the search and solution space to better help us to design effective pruning rules (for refining search space of a branch in Section~\ref{subsec:progressive_bound}) and branching strategies (for reducing the number of formed branches in Section~\ref{subsec:Sym-PivSE}). In fact, our new algorithm \texttt{FastQC} designed based on TDim would have the worst-case time complexity strictly smaller than that of \texttt{Quick+} (details will be introduced in Section~\ref{subsec:summary_fastqc}).
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/framework5.tex:656:% Consider a branch $B=(S,C,D)$, we observe that Equation~(\ref{eq:search_space_bound}) gives a loose boundary of the search space since it assumes the size of a possible $\gamma$-QC up to $|S\cup C|$ (note that this boundary is reached only when $G[S\cup C]$ is a $\gamma$-QC). Hence, it rarely helps to design pruning rules which refine branch $B$ physically, e.g., removing some vertices from $C$. To improve, we propose a progressive bounded method which iteratively and alternatively tightens the theoretical search space of $B$ under TDim (i.e., by a smaller square region) and the physical branch $(S,C,D)$ (i.e., by pruning rules). Below, we elaborate on the details of this method.
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/framework5.tex:659:% \noindent\textbf{Tighten theoretical search space under TDim.} Let $H_m(B)$ be the $\gamma$-QC with the largest size to be found in $B$ and $k_m(B)=k(|H_m(B)|)$ denote the maximum number of disconnections allowed for each vertex in a $\gamma$-QC with size $|H_m(B)|$. We observe that any $\gamma$-QC $G[H]$ in $B$ would have $|H|\leq |H_m(B)|$ and thus $\overline{\delta}_m(H)\leq k(|H|) \leq k(|H_m(B)|) = k_m(B)$, which figures out a tight square region $(|S|,\overline{\delta}_m(S))\times (|H_m(B)|,k_m(B))$ that contains the solution space, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:TD_example}(b).
/tmp/arxiv2305.14047.tNm2tP/framework5.tex:672:%         |H_u(B)|=\min \{|S\cup C|, d_{min}(B)/\gamma + 1\},\  k_u(B)= k(|H_u(B)|).
