index 70164fd359ccb27e8145df2e270873b69da08f35..e39702b4a946acf3632d52da2c7a3b7e504f16ed
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -1,125 +1,156 @@
-# Large induced quasi-cliques from arXiv:2305.14047
+# Maximal affine-line quasi-cliques from arXiv:2305.14047
 
-> **Status:** the generator passes every local gate, but this directory is not
-> submission-ready. The required OpenRouter run aborted because the configured
-> key has exhausted its total limit. The script-written error transcripts are
-> retained; no oracle error is counted as a failed solve.
+> **Status:** the module and every local gate pass. The required external oracle
+> verdict is unavailable: OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “total limit exceeded”
+> on every bare, structural-hint, and placebo redraw. These script-written error
+> records are retained and are not counted as failed solver attempts. This
+> directory is therefore complete locally but **not submission-ready**.
 
 ## Profile
 
 | field | value |
 |---|---|
-| track | **A — structural hardness** |
+| track | **B — no-tool compression** |
 | native domain | combinatorics |
-| object regime | finite discrete |
-| computational core | graph |
-| certificate form | integer tuple |
-| intuition | constraint propagation through the disconnection/complement view |
+| object regime | finite field |
+| computational core | linear algebra |
+| certificate form | 2-row matrix certificate |
+| intuition | invariant: constraint rows share a polynomial root |
 | domain essentiality | native |
 | reduction | none |
 
-## Problem and provenance
+## Problem and trust model
 
 The source is Yu and Long, [*Fast Maximal Quasi-clique Enumeration: A
 Pruning and Branching Co-Design Approach*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14047).
-A solver receives an undirected graph and must return exactly 21 vertices whose
-induced subgraph is connected and has minimum degree 18. This is precisely a
-`9/10`-quasi-clique under Definition 1 in Section 2.1. Checking a witness only
-requires exact adjacency counts and a connectivity traversal.
+Section 2.1 defines induced `gamma`-quasi-cliques and explicitly notes that a
+1-quasi-clique is a clique. The solver receives a succinct, exact Cayley graph
+on all vectors of `F_q^d` and must return an affine line whose `q` points form a
+maximal 1-quasi-clique. The answer is the canonical matrix `[base, direction]`.
 
-The answer is known by inverse generation. The generator first samples 21
-uniform vertex labels, then makes their induced graph `K_21` with one randomly
-ordered Hamiltonian cycle deleted. Every planted vertex therefore has degree
-18. Only afterward are background edges sampled. The cross-edge probability is
-chosen so that planted and background vertices have the same expected total
-degree, suppressing the simplest planting signature.
+Generation is inverse: a field root `a` is sampled first, the direction
+`(1,a,...,a^(d-1))` is retained, and every displayed constraint row is built as
+the coefficient list of a polynomial divisible by `X-a`. Thus the direction is
+in their common kernel without solving a generated system. The graph connects
+every nonzero kernel difference. For every outside vertex, its syndrome is
+constant along the line while `z0` runs through all of `F_q`; one point hits the
+explicit forbidden value `z0=c·s`. Therefore no outside vertex extends the
+clique. `verify` executes these modular checks and never reads `inst["answer"]`.
 
-This deliberately does **not** claim to certify maximality. Section 2.1 says
-exact maximality checking is NP-hard, and Section 4.5 calls the test “no single
-outside vertex extends the set” only a *necessary* condition. Because
-quasi-cliques are non-hereditary, that test is not an exact maximality witness.
-The family instead asks for one paper-native large quasi-clique, not the full
-MQCE enumeration output.
+## Why Track B
 
-## Why Track A
+This is not an average-case hardness claim. Section 3 gives Quick+ worst-case
+time `O*(2^N)` and Theorem 1 gives FastQC time `O(N d alpha_k^N)`. The paper’s
+Section 6 also says larger `gamma` and `theta` reduce running time, while dense
+graphs weaken degree pruning; using `gamma=1` is deliberately an easy special
+case of the general QC definition.
 
-Section 2.1 establishes NP-hardness of MQCE, while Theorem 1 bounds FastQC by
-`O(n d alpha_k^n)` with `alpha_k < 2`. At shipping parameters `n=384`,
-`gamma=0.9`, and `theta=21`, the paper's `tau(n)` parameter is 39, so the stated
-base is already close to 2. Section 6 also reports that denser graphs weaken
-degree pruning and raise running time. These results motivate—but do not prove
-hardness of—this generated distribution. The distributional evidence is the
-measured panel: six attacks each failed on 8/8 seeds, including centered
-spectral recovery, a construction-aware complement-cycle hill climb, and an
-exact fixed-deficiency branch-and-bound search capped at 250,000 nodes. The
-last attack consumed a median 250,011 visited calls and 2.638 seconds.
+The difficulty here is compression. At shipping size the graph has 62,748,517
+vertices and 5,229,043 projective directions. The successful mechanical
+reference solver satisfies one linear constraint and enumerates the rest: over
+8 seeds it checked a median 239,295 lines, used 6,740,125 exact field operations,
+and took 0.219 s. Reading each row as polynomial coefficients exposes the short
+route: scan 13 possible common roots by Horner evaluation and write the
+geometric direction. That succeeded 8/8 in 108 median operations (150 maximum,
+11 microseconds median). A machine does either; a no-tool solver must notice the
+second representation and execute at most 162 small modular operations.
 
 ## Worked demo
 
-The hand-scale preset is `make_instance(n=12, k=5, seed=0)`, with `gamma=1/2`:
+`make_instance(n=2, q=3, seed=0)` renders in full as:
 
 ```text
-0: 1 2 4 5 6 10
-1: 0 2 3 6 7 8 9
-2: 0 1 3 4 6 8 9 11
-3: 1 2 4 7 8
-4: 0 2 3 5 7 8 9
-5: 0 4 9 11
-6: 0 1 2 8 11
-7: 1 3 4 9 11
-8: 1 2 3 4 6 11
